userStories.description:
**Goal** — Add a policy-faithful confined test CLI, nax-agent test:confined integration, and portable nax-agent fixtures.

**Motivation** — Outside the sandbox the same tree is fully green.

**Approach** — **Confined test runner (nax#2523).** A nax-agent script runs `bun test <paths>` inside the same OS sandbox a confined agent session gets. It builds the policy with `buildSandboxPolicy({ confined: true, ... })`, wraps the command with the srt backend, and exports `TMPDIR`/`TMP`/`TEMP` (the session temp dir under the run's own temp root) plus `NAX_TEST_CONFINED=1`. It is the regression gate for test temp hygiene: a test that writes under a literal `/tmp/...` path fails there exactly as it fails in an agent session, while a test that uses `makeTempDir()` (which resolves through `os.tmpdir()`, and so through `$TMPDIR`) passes. Each package gets a `test:confined` script, and CI runs it over `test/unit/` and `test/integration/` after the existing bubblewrap install step. The runner lives in `packages/nax-agent/scripts/`, not `packages/repo-tooling/scripts/`, because it imports nax-agent's own `src/sandbox` (repo-tooling may not import a package's `src/`).

The file lists in US-001, US-002 and US-003 are exhaustive for main `0bf6cef38`: the census ran every file under `test/unit/` and `test/integration/` of both packages inside the confined sandbox. A failure a story finds outside its list (a test added since) is fixed the same way, within the same story.

Failing tests are fixed by class:
- **Class A — the test makes its own temp dir or lock file under a literal `/tmp/...`.** Use `makeTempDir()` / `withTempDir()` / `cleanupTempDir()` from the package's `test/helpers`.
- **Class B — a "fake" fixture path the code under test actually writes** (for example `prdPath: "/tmp/test-prd.json"` reaching `savePRD`, which writes `/tmp/test-prd.json.tmp-<uuid>`). Point the fixture at a real temp dir, or stub the write through the module's existing `_deps` seam. These writes also leak into the host's `/tmp` on every ordinary run today.
- **Class C — the behaviour itself needs something a confined sandbox withholds** (a nested sandbox, a detached process probe, the shared `/tmp/nax` parent). Fix the test when its expectation is merely environment-dependent. Otherwise mark it `test.skipIf(isConfinedTestRun())`, with a comment that names what the sandbox withholds.

The `.nax/scratchpad/` directory is the agent's own notes space, written through the Scratchpad tools. It is not a home for test fixtures: tests run on hosts and in CI too, and the test-side counterpart of the sandbox's own "use $TMPDIR or .nax/scratchpad/, not /tmp" guidance is `$TMPDIR`.

**Interface** — New in nax-agent, in `packages/nax-agent/scripts/lib/confined-run.ts`:
```ts
export const CONFINED_TEST_ENV = "NAX_TEST_CONFINED";
export const SANDBOX_UNAVAILABLE_EXIT = 3;
export const USAGE_EXIT = 2;

export function confinedTestPolicy(input: {
  repoRoot: string; runTmpRoot: string; home: string; platform: NodeJS.Platform;
}): Promise<SandboxPolicy>;            // buildSandboxPolicy({ confined: true, tempRoots: runTempRoots(...), projectStateDir: ".nax", ... })

export function confinedTestCommand(input: {
  packageDir: string; tmpDir: string; paths: readonly string[]; timeoutMs: number;
}): string;                            // "export TMPDIR=… TMP=… TEMP=… NAX_TEST_CONFINED=1; cd <pkg> && bun test <paths> --timeout=<timeoutMs>"

export function probeConfinedSandbox(): Promise<ProbeResult>;
export function runConfinedTests(input: { packageDir: string; paths: readonly string[]; timeoutMs?: number }): Promise<number>;  // timeoutMs default 60_000
export const _confinedRunDeps: { backend; probe; gitRoot; spawn; mkdir; rm; tmpdir; homedir; platform; stdout; stderr };
// runConfinedTests: repoRoot = (await gitRoot(packageDir)) ?? packageDir; runTmpRoot = /tmp/nax/confined-<pid>-<random>;
// session temp dir = <runTmpRoot>/s; gitGuardFiles = listGitGuardFiles(...), credentialFiles = listCredentialFiles(...).
// spawn uses runtimeSpawn with stdin and stderr "inherit" and stdout "pipe" (AgentSpawnOptions.stdout is "pipe" only);
// the piped stdout is copied chunk by chunk to _confinedRunDeps.stdout (process.stdout by default).
```

`packages/nax-agent/scripts/run-confined-tests.ts` is the CLI over it: `bun scripts/run-confined-tests.ts [--package=<dir>] [--timeout=<ms>] [--probe] <test paths...>`. Argument checks come first: without `--probe` and with no test path the CLI exits 2 before any probe. `--probe` runs `probeConfinedSandbox()` only (the srt backend built from `DEFAULT_SANDBOX_CONFIG.network`): exit 0 when available, otherwise exit 3 with `sandbox unavailable: <reason>` on stderr.

Each `test:confined` script mirrors its package's normal test phases as separate runner invocations, so a confined-only failure is never an artefact of mixing directories in one `bun test` process:
- nax-agent: `bun scripts/run-confined-tests.ts ./test/unit/ && bun scripts/run-confined-tests.ts ./test/integration/` (60 s per test, as its `test` script uses).
- nax: `bun ../nax-agent/scripts/run-confined-tests.ts --package=. --timeout=5000 ./test/unit/ && bun ../nax-agent/scripts/run-confined-tests.ts --package=. --timeout=5000 ./test/integration/` (5 s per test, as `scripts/run-tests-phases.ts` uses). The nax script is US-002's work.

The wall-clock bound in CI is the job's `timeout-minutes`; each story raises its job's value if the added confined step needs it.

The two confined fixtures are named `*.fixture.ts`, not `*.test.ts`, under `test/fixtures/confined/`: the runner passes them as explicit `./` paths, which `bun test` runs whatever the suffix, while the package's normal `bun test ./test/unit/ ./test/integration/` never collects them (`.nax/rules/forbidden-patterns-tests.md`).

Implement confinedTestPolicy from buildSandboxPolicy, runTempRoots, resolveGitLayout, listNaxEntries, listGitGuardFiles, listCredentialFiles, DEFAULT_SANDBOX_CONFIG and EMPTY_OWNED_PATHS_POLICY; keep realOrRaw canonicalization and the Claude settings deny. Add isConfinedTestRun() to test/helpers/confined.ts and export it through the existing test helper barrel. Fix test/unit/internal/file-lock.test.ts and test/unit/internal/path-file-lock.test.ts (class A, `/tmp/nax-*-lock-test-*` dirs onto makeTempDir()), and test/unit/runtime/node-runtime.test.ts (class C, the detached-process case skipped in a confined run). Keep every assertion's meaning and do not change SPAWN_CASES. Add 'Test (confined sandbox)' to CI's nax-agent job after its bubblewrap install, without changing existing script/step behavior.

Failure handling: No usable sandbox backend: runConfinedTests returns SANDBOX_UNAVAILABLE_EXIT (3), writes `sandbox unavailable: <reason>` to stderr, runs no tests and never falls back unsandboxed. Removing the runner's temp root fails: ignored; the exit code is the test run's. No test path: exit USAGE_EXIT (2), usage text on stderr. packageDir outside a git work tree: build with repoRoot equal to packageDir. Confined-runner integration tests on a host with no sandbox, or inside a confined run: skipped (no nested sandbox). Invoke commandFinished then reset after the spawned command and remove the run root even on a nonzero exit.

**Scope** — In: nax-agent runner, CLI, helper, seven declared new files, three nax-agent fixture repairs, package.json script and root CI nax-agent step. Out: nax unit hygiene/helper/script (US-002), nax integration hygiene/CI step (US-003), PARTIAL findings (US-004), telemetry/publish logs (US-005), review gate prompts (US-006). Changing the confined-session sandbox policy itself (the `/tmp` deny, the run temp roots, network rules) — this spec makes tests fit the policy, never the reverse. Feeding the confined runner's result into nax's own run gates or the workflow `full-suite` gate — it is a CI and developer gate only. Making `ps` or other setuid programs runnable inside the macOS sandbox, and making a sandbox launch inside another sandbox work — tests that need either are skipped in a confined run instead. Running the confined runner over `test/ui/` or `test/e2e/` — only `test/unit/` and `test/integration/` are covered. A static lint for literal `/tmp/` strings in tests — most such strings are harmless path values that are never written; the confined CI run is the gate. Moving test fixtures into `.nax/scratchpad/` — the scratchpad is the agent's own notes space; tests use `$TMPDIR` through `makeTempDir()`.

**Goal** — Preserve a partially resolved defect on its original finding id and suppress exact-location residual duplicates in story and branch re-review.

**Motivation** — One defect became two critical items and two advisor rulings.

**Approach** — **PARTIAL re-review verdict (nax#2524).** Both re-review prompts (story and branch) offer a third verdict, `PARTIAL`, which must carry a `residual`. `parseReReviewReply` keeps the residual. A shared `absorbReReviewVerdict` keeps the same finding id open with its issue replaced by the residual, and drops a new finding that restates the residual at the same file and line. A `PARTIAL` with no usable residual fails closed to `NOT_ADDRESSED`.

**Interface** — `ReReviewVerdict` in packages/nax/src/operations/workflow-types.ts: each verdict entry gains `readonly residual?: ReReviewResidual`, where `ReReviewResidual = { readonly issue: string; readonly suggestion?: string; readonly file?: string; readonly line?: number }` is a new exported interface in the same file.
```ts
export interface ReReviewResidual {
  readonly issue: string;
  readonly suggestion?: string;
  readonly file?: string;
  readonly line?: number;
}
export interface ReReviewVerdict {
  readonly verdicts: readonly { readonly id: string; readonly addressed: boolean; readonly note: string; readonly residual?: ReReviewResidual }[];
  readonly newFindings: readonly WorkflowFindingDraft[];
}
export function absorbReReviewVerdict(asked: readonly WorkflowFinding[], verdict: ReReviewVerdict): { open: readonly WorkflowFinding[]; newDrafts: readonly WorkflowFindingDraft[] };
```

`parseReReviewReply(output, openIds)` — Target: a `verdict` of `"PARTIAL"` whose `residual` is an object with a non-empty string `issue` yields `addressed: false` plus `residual`; any other `verdict` value yields no `residual` key. `parseReReviewReply` residual sanitising follows `toDraft`'s field rules: `issue` must be a non-empty string; `suggestion` and `file` are kept only as non-empty strings; `line` is kept only as a positive integer.

`runStoryLoop` re-review absorption (packages/nax/src/workflow/story-loop.ts, the `stillOpen` block) and `branchReReviewStep` (packages/nax/src/workflow/feature-machine.ts) — Target: both call the new pure `absorbReReviewVerdict(asked, verdict)` exported from the new `packages/nax/src/workflow/re-review-absorb.ts` (no `@/operations` or `@/prompts` imports), which returns `{ open: readonly WorkflowFinding[]; newDrafts: readonly WorkflowFindingDraft[] }`:
- `open` is `asked` in order, minus every finding whose verdict is `addressed: true`; a finding whose verdict has a `residual` is replaced by `{ ...finding, issue: residual.issue }`, with `suggestion`, `file` and `line` taken from the residual when present and kept from the finding otherwise, and with no `observed` key.
- `newDrafts` is `verdict.newFindings` minus each draft whose `file` and `line` both equal a residual finding's effective `file` and `line` (the residual's value, else the original finding's). When either effective value is absent, nothing is dropped. A dropped draft reaches neither `reviewOpen`, the deferred minors, `newMinors` nor the ledger; the artifacts keep today's shape: `rereview-<R>.json` holds the parsed verdict, and `branch-rereview.json` holds it with `newFindings` replaced by the assigned blocking findings plus the non-blocking drafts, both taken from `newDrafts`.
- `reReviewText` in `feature-machine.ts` gains the verdicts as a parameter so it can print `<id> PARTIAL`; for an item without a residual, both re-review summaries keep today's text byte for byte (pinned by `story-loop-log.test.ts` and `feature-machine-log.test.ts`).
- `runStoryLoop` passes its `reviewOpen` as `asked`. `branchReReviewStep` passes `rec.reviewOpen` only (never the fix-now minors in its `items`), sets `reviewOpen` to `open` plus the newly assigned blocking drafts, and keeps its existing verdict-based `fix-now` → `leave` handling for minors. Preserve existing second branch fix eligibility and its artifacts too.

`buildWorkflowReReviewPrompt` (`workflow-re-review-builder.ts`) and `buildWorkflowBranchReReviewPrompt` (`workflow-branch-builder.ts`) — Target: the reply contract reads `{"findings": [{"id": "F1", "verdict": "ADDRESSED|PARTIAL|NOT_ADDRESSED", "note": "one sentence", "residual": {"issue": "...", "suggestion": "...", "file": "path", "line": 0}}], "newFindings": [...]}` (`B1` in the branch prompt).

Re-review instruction, both prompts. It replaces "For each finding id, answer ADDRESSED or NOT_ADDRESSED.":
> For each finding id, answer ADDRESSED, PARTIAL or NOT_ADDRESSED. PARTIAL means the fix resolved part of the finding and a smaller problem remains: describe only what remains in "residual". Never list a residual again in "newFindings".

Change packages/nax/test/unit/prompts/workflow-branch-builder.test.ts's existing two-value assertion to `ADDRESSED|PARTIAL|NOT_ADDRESSED`. Extend parser, story-loop, feature-machine and log tests, plus new pure absorption tests. A missing/empty residual keeps the finding open unchanged; unknown/non-PARTIAL values have no residual.

**Scope** — In: optional residual type/parser, new pure absorption helper, both consumer state machines, re-review prompt contracts and PARTIAL done-text. Out: confined hygiene (US-001..US-003), telemetry/publish progress (US-005), normal review gate-result prompts (US-006). An LLM or fuzzy-match dedupe of re-review new findings — only the exact file-and-line match against a `PARTIAL` residual is dropped.

**Goal** — Carry nonzero cache usage through workflow role telemetry and expose CI waits and publishing sub-steps without changing results.

**Motivation** — The publish step ran 4m 56s with no log line at any level between `final-gates green` and `publish draft <url>`.

**Approach** — **Cache tokens (nax#2525).** `RoleTokens` gains optional `cacheReadTokens` and `cacheWriteTokens`. `startTokenMeter` and `addRoleTokens` carry them, emitting each field only when it is non-zero, so every existing role-entry shape is unchanged when there is no cache traffic. The workflow summary's role line appends `cache r <n> w <n>` when either is non-zero. `inputTokens` keeps its meaning (uncached input).

**Publish progress (nax#2526).** `waitForChecks` logs a start line, a line whenever the check summary changes, a heartbeat at least every 60 seconds while waiting, and an end line. `prMode` / `pushMode` / `finishPr` log each publish sub-step.

**Interface** — `RoleTokens` in packages/nax/src/workflow/types.ts adds `readonly cacheReadTokens?: number; readonly cacheWriteTokens?: number`. `MeterEvent.tokenUsage` in tokens.ts gains the same two optional fields; `WorkflowRoleUsage` in packages/nax/src/log-format/types.ts gains the same two optional fields.
```ts
interface RoleTokens {
  readonly inputTokens: number;
  readonly outputTokens: number;
  readonly costUsd: number;
  readonly calls: number;
  readonly cacheReadTokens?: number;
  readonly cacheWriteTokens?: number;
}
export const CI_WAIT_HEARTBEAT_MS = 60_000;
```
`waitForChecks(forge, prRef, opts)` — Target: same signature and results, plus the progress log lines. A new exported constant `CI_WAIT_HEARTBEAT_MS = 60_000` sits in the same file. Existing signature:
```ts
export async function waitForChecks(
  forge: ForgeKind,
  prRef: string,
  opts: { readonly cwd: string; readonly timeoutMs: number; readonly pollMs: number; readonly signal?: AbortSignal },
): Promise<CiWaitResult>;
```
Carry fields through startTokenMeter, addRoleTokens and buildWorkflowRunSummary to formatWorkflowSummary's roleLine. Legacy records lacking either cache field are read as zero; no zero-valued key is emitted and uncached input/cost/call meanings stay unchanged.

Publish log lines (stage `workflow`, data `storyId: "_run"` first):
- `CI wait: <prRef> (timeout <timeoutMs>ms, poll <pollMs>ms)`
- `CI checks: <passed>/<total> passed, <pending> pending` plus `, failing: <names>` when any check failed (GitHub; `passed` counts the `pass` and `skipping` buckets, the same set `classifyGh` treats as green); `CI pipeline: <status>` (GitLab)
- `CI wait: still waiting after <elapsed> (<last summary>)`
- `CI wait done: <green|failed|pending> after <elapsed>`
- `Publish: pushing <branch>` / `Publish: pushed <branch>` / `Publish: draft PR <url>` / `Publish: PR marked ready <url>` / `Publish: PR kept as draft <url>: <reasons joined with "; ">`

`<elapsed>` uses the existing `formatDuration`. Log at info for progress, changed summaries only (not every unchanged poll), heartbeat once at least 60000ms have passed since the last info line, and end upon a terminal return. With no logger installed during the CI wait, progress lines are skipped; waitForChecks returns as before. Preserve GitHub/GitLab classification, timeout and CLI-error pending behavior, abort behavior, readiness rules and the existing return objects. No successful pushed line is emitted after a failed push; use URLs returned by the existing forge operations. Keep draft reasons in order joined by `; `. Tests use _ciWaitDeps clocks/sleep/run and finalize.test.ts wire() forge fakes; no real minute-long test waits.

**Scope** — In: workflow role field propagation/presentation and CI-wait/main publish progress for pr/push modes. Out: confinement (US-001..US-003), PARTIAL verdicts (US-004), gate-context prompts (US-006). Splitting `inputTokens` into cached and uncached parts, or changing cost computation — the cache fields are additive telemetry. Cache token columns in the cost ledger, `run.complete` or metrics files other than the workflow role table and its summary line. Log lines for `workflow.publish` mode `none`, and progress lines for the ledger commit and ledger push. Configuring the CI-wait heartbeat interval — it is the constant `CI_WAIT_HEARTBEAT_MS`.
